            <ArticleTitle>The painful shoulder: part I. Clinical evaluation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3079-88</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Family physicians need to understand diagnostic and treatment strategies for common causes of shoulder pain. We review key elements of the history and physical examination and describe maneuvers that can be used to reach an appropriate diagnosis. Examination of the shoulder should include inspection, palpation, evaluation of range of motion and provocative testing. In addition, a thorough sensorimotor examination of the upper extremity should be performed, and the neck and elbow should be evaluated.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
